This code provides a basic framework for interfacing with the Adafruit APDS9960 sensor on a Raspberry Pi 4 Model B using the libgpiod library. It initializes the I2C bus, sets up the sensor, and enters a loop to poll the sensor every 100ms. When a proximity event is detected, it reads the raw RGB color values and appends a line containing the timestamp, the three RGB components, and the proximity value to a CSV file named `color_proximity_log.csv`. The program handles I2C communication errors by closing and reopening the bus and re-initializing the sensor, and it catches SIGINT and SIGTERM signals to perform a graceful shutdown that closes the I2C handle, finalizes the CSV file, and releases any resources allocated by the driver. Note that the `apds9960_read_proximity` and `apds9960_read_rgb` functions are assumed to be part of a custom library for interfacing with the APDS9960 sensor, and you will need to implement these functions or use an existing library that provides this functionality.

