This C program for a Raspberry Pi 4 Model B reads weight from an HX711 load cell amplifier using a bit-banged GPIO bus, applies clock high/low delays of at least 1 µs, and obtains raw 24-bit signed values. It prompts the user to place a known weight at startup to compute and save a calibration factor to a file named `calibration.txt`. The program continuously samples the sensor, treats a data line that remains high for more than 100 µs as a communication timeout, and when the calibrated weight exceeds a threshold of 5.0 kg, it engages a solenoid door lock connected to GPIO27 (BCM) by setting the pin high for exactly 5 seconds before releasing it. It logs the timestamp and weight to a file called `security.log` each time the threshold is exceeded. The program robustly handles driver initialization failures, file open or write errors, calibration read errors, and HX711 timeouts. A SIGINT handler is provided to gracefully unlock the solenoid, terminate the driver, close any open files, and release all resources on any exit path.

